1
00:00:17,351 --> 00:00:19,353
[breathing heavily]

2
00:00:26,985 --> 00:00:29,988
[panting]

3
00:00:46,547 --> 00:00:47,589
Hey!

4
00:00:49,174 --> 00:00:52,009
Hey! Hey! Hey! Hey!

5
00:00:52,010 --> 00:00:55,264
- [groans, panting]
- Hey! Hey!

6
00:00:57,266 --> 00:00:59,308
- What the fuck was that?
- [Lukas] What?

7
00:00:59,309 --> 00:01:01,060
Did they say anything over the radio?

8
00:01:01,061 --> 00:01:04,312
[stammers] I don't... I don't know.

9
00:01:04,313 --> 00:01:06,357
- I don't know. It's not working.
- Fuck! You're shot.

10
00:01:06,358 --> 00:01:07,650
- What?
- You don't feel that?

11
00:01:07,651 --> 00:01:11,696
- Oh, fuck. Help me up. [groans]
- Ah, shit. It went through the radio!

12
00:01:11,697 --> 00:01:13,197
- Come on, we gotta go...
- Oh, fuck.

13
00:01:13,198 --> 00:01:15,409
You're all right. You're all right.
Come on, come on. You're okay.

14
00:01:20,831 --> 00:01:22,248
- [Lukas grunts]
- Watch your step there.

15
00:01:22,249 --> 00:01:24,041
- [Lukas] Yeah.
- One step at a time, all right?

16
00:01:24,042 --> 00:01:25,127
[Lukas] I'm okay.

17
00:01:29,006 --> 00:01:30,006
[groans]

18
00:01:30,007 --> 00:01:31,383
[Kennedy] Come on. Come on.

19
00:01:32,217 --> 00:01:33,594
[Lukas groans] Fuck.

20
00:01:34,178 --> 00:01:35,678
- Okay?
- I'm good. I'm good.

21
00:01:35,679 --> 00:01:36,763
Get the door.

22
00:01:40,934 --> 00:01:42,394
Hey!

23
00:01:45,564 --> 00:01:46,565
Hey!

24
00:01:47,482 --> 00:01:50,026
- [Lukas grunts]
- [Kennedy] Let us in!

25
00:01:50,027 --> 00:01:51,445
Ah, fuck!

26
00:01:58,202 --> 00:01:59,870
[grunts]

27
00:02:04,374 --> 00:02:06,627
Hey! Hey!

28
00:02:07,628 --> 00:02:09,044
Who are you, and what do you want?

29
00:02:09,045 --> 00:02:11,589
- [Kennedy] Juliette sent us!
- [Lukas] Open the door!

30
00:02:11,590 --> 00:02:13,508
How do I know
you're not covered in poison?

31
00:02:13,509 --> 00:02:14,967
[Lukas] Because I was shot.

32
00:02:14,968 --> 00:02:17,386
If there was any poison on me,
it would've gotten through the holes.

33
00:02:17,387 --> 00:02:19,515
- I would be dead already.
- Open the fucking door!

34
00:02:20,390 --> 00:02:22,642
I got you. I got you. You ready?

35
00:02:22,643 --> 00:02:24,228
It's okay. Come on.

36
00:02:25,354 --> 00:02:26,730
[grunts]

37
00:02:28,065 --> 00:02:30,566
Help me. [pants]

38
00:02:30,567 --> 00:02:31,943
[sighs]

39
00:02:31,944 --> 00:02:36,114
[Jimmy] Okay, so, the bullet
went through the radio and then him.

40
00:02:37,449 --> 00:02:39,618
- [sighs]
- Okay. All right.

41
00:02:45,624 --> 00:02:47,668
[panting] Okay.

42
00:02:50,003 --> 00:02:51,296
Is Juliette okay?

43
00:02:52,840 --> 00:02:55,299
Yeah, of course she is.
She's safe and sound at home.

44
00:02:55,300 --> 00:03:00,973
We're the one's getting shot at
by some fucking thing in the sky.

45
00:03:01,473 --> 00:03:04,141
[pants] Okay. Put-- Put pressure.

46
00:03:04,142 --> 00:03:05,561
[Audrey] Okay.

47
00:03:16,488 --> 00:03:17,656
[electricity crackling]

48
00:03:23,787 --> 00:03:24,997
You know what you're doing?

49
00:03:26,748 --> 00:03:28,333
[stammers] The book does.

50
00:03:45,434 --> 00:03:47,102
[clock chimes, rattles]

51
00:04:26,808 --> 00:04:28,769
- [radio activates]
- [gasps] Hey.

52
00:04:29,561 --> 00:04:31,104
- [static on radio]
- [chuckles]

53
00:04:31,605 --> 00:04:32,605
Go get the other guy.

54
00:04:32,606 --> 00:04:33,815
[sighs]

55
00:04:34,691 --> 00:04:35,734
[chuckles]

56
00:04:41,073 --> 00:04:42,532
- Hey.
- [Kennedy] You fix it?

57
00:04:42,533 --> 00:04:43,616
- [static on radio]
- Yeah.

58
00:04:43,617 --> 00:04:45,451
Do you wanna... Do you wanna talk to them?

59
00:04:45,452 --> 00:04:46,536
- Me?
- Yeah.

60
00:04:46,537 --> 00:04:49,580
No, no, no, no. You gotta talk in code.
It wasn't supposed to be my job.

61
00:04:49,581 --> 00:04:51,040
- No, I... [stammers]
- Do it. [exclaims]

62
00:04:51,041 --> 00:04:53,459
- Do it. Press it.
- I got it. I got it. I got it.

63
00:04:53,460 --> 00:04:54,545
[Kennedy] <i>Hey.</i>

64
00:04:55,712 --> 00:04:57,256
Yeah. Donny, what is it?

65
00:04:59,049 --> 00:05:00,342
Who the fuck is Donny?

66
00:05:01,718 --> 00:05:02,719
Who is this?

67
00:05:04,596 --> 00:05:06,807
[whispers] Oh, fuck. My name.
I don't remember my name.

68
00:05:07,516 --> 00:05:09,100
[mutters indistinctly, inhales sharply]

69
00:05:09,101 --> 00:05:14,022
This is Deputy Calso, um,
here on, uh... Level 17.

70
00:05:16,358 --> 00:05:19,694
[Juliette] <i>Uh, Calso this is, um,
Deputy...</i> [stammers] <i>...Crane.</i>

71
00:05:19,695 --> 00:05:21,571
- Juliette?
- <i>Please continue.</i>

72
00:05:21,572 --> 00:05:22,780
Deputy Crane? Yeah, okay.

73
00:05:22,781 --> 00:05:25,116
So... So, uh, we had a-a little bit
of a situation here,

74
00:05:25,117 --> 00:05:26,659
but, um, the... the kids are all right.

75
00:05:26,660 --> 00:05:29,620
[Martha] <i>Donny.
Donny, do you hear me? This is Dispatch.</i>

76
00:05:29,621 --> 00:05:33,791
<i>Your delivery is no longer necessary.
Please return to base.</i>

77
00:05:33,792 --> 00:05:35,168
Who-- Who's Donny?

78
00:05:35,169 --> 00:05:37,587
<i>Oh. Calso will you
get off the line, please? Shut up.</i>

79
00:05:37,588 --> 00:05:39,463
- Shut up?
- <i>Donny, did you get that?</i>

80
00:05:39,464 --> 00:05:42,466
<i>Your delivery is no longer necessary.</i>

81
00:05:42,467 --> 00:05:44,344
<i>Please return to base.</i>

82
00:05:44,845 --> 00:05:46,555
[Teddy] <i>This is Donny... It's off.</i>

83
00:05:47,556 --> 00:05:48,682
<i>Returning to base.</i>

84
00:05:50,767 --> 00:05:51,976
[exhales]

85
00:05:51,977 --> 00:05:54,979
Calso. C-Calso, this is, um, Crane.

86
00:05:54,980 --> 00:05:56,564
[stammers] Can you hear me?

87
00:05:56,565 --> 00:05:57,899
[Kennedy] <i>Hey.</i>

88
00:05:57,900 --> 00:05:58,983
Hey.

89
00:05:58,984 --> 00:06:03,446
<i>Really good work on Level 17.
Is Deputy, um, James there?</i>

90
00:06:03,447 --> 00:06:04,822
- That's you.
- What?

91
00:06:04,823 --> 00:06:06,116
You-- You gotta talk in code.

92
00:06:06,700 --> 00:06:08,869
- I don't know how-- [stammers]
- You gotta do it.

93
00:06:11,955 --> 00:06:13,789
- This is Deputy Jimmy...
- Code.

94
00:06:13,790 --> 00:06:14,875
...James.

95
00:06:16,168 --> 00:06:21,464
Hi. I haven't seen you since, um...
since I transferred.

96
00:06:21,465 --> 00:06:24,593
How's the, um... How's the family?
Are they... [stammers] Is everyone okay?

97
00:06:26,220 --> 00:06:29,389
Um... My family,
th-they're doing pretty good.

98
00:06:31,058 --> 00:06:32,141
Okay, great.

99
00:06:32,142 --> 00:06:35,938
And how... Turley...
How's-- How's, um, Deputy Turley?

100
00:06:36,522 --> 00:06:37,940
[Jimmy] <i>Turley?</i>

101
00:06:38,440 --> 00:06:42,193
I got it. Yeah, it's all right.
Deputy Turley had a little bit of a fall.

102
00:06:42,194 --> 00:06:44,363
But he is, um... he's okay.

103
00:06:45,531 --> 00:06:46,740
Okay.

104
00:06:47,282 --> 00:06:49,952
Okay, I'm glad to hear, um...
[inhales deeply]

105
00:06:51,203 --> 00:06:54,289
<i>James, you remember
telling me about your mom?</i>

106
00:06:56,083 --> 00:06:59,460
<i>I'm gonna need you to open up an
investigation into what happened to her.</i>

107
00:06:59,461 --> 00:07:02,839
<i>I wish I could tell you why,
but I don't have time so, um,</i>

108
00:07:02,840 --> 00:07:04,883
<i>Calso will fill you in. Okay?</i>

109
00:10:01,393 --> 00:10:04,312
The digger you saw was the prototype.

110
00:10:04,313 --> 00:10:05,980
Nine more are being built.

111
00:10:05,981 --> 00:10:09,358
Ten diggers to dig
five additional silos each.

112
00:10:09,359 --> 00:10:10,944
Fifty silos in total.

113
00:10:11,445 --> 00:10:13,071
What are the silos for?

114
00:10:17,451 --> 00:10:18,492
Seeds.

115
00:10:18,493 --> 00:10:21,163
Like the seed bank in Svalbard?

116
00:10:22,289 --> 00:10:23,456
[Anna] In a way.

117
00:10:23,457 --> 00:10:25,458
Sorry. Why ten diggers?

118
00:10:25,459 --> 00:10:28,377
So we can dig ten silos at the same time.

119
00:10:28,378 --> 00:10:30,296
Once the digging is complete,

120
00:10:30,297 --> 00:10:33,217
we'll pull the diggers out,
and move them on to the next ten.

121
00:10:33,967 --> 00:10:36,135
In the Army Corps we had a joke...

122
00:10:36,136 --> 00:10:39,597
Does the engineer see
the glass as half full or half empty?

123
00:10:39,598 --> 00:10:42,308
Well, I'm guessing
that the engineer is an optimist.

124
00:10:42,309 --> 00:10:43,559
Half full.

125
00:10:43,560 --> 00:10:46,522
The engineer sees the glass as twice
as big as it needs to be. [chuckles]

126
00:10:48,482 --> 00:10:51,985
- [stammers] You need more diggers.
- How many more?

127
00:10:52,528 --> 00:10:55,614
Well, you want 50 silos.
You need 50 diggers.

128
00:10:57,574 --> 00:11:01,327
Well, I am rich, Daniel, but 50 diggers...

129
00:11:01,328 --> 00:11:03,329
Reusing the diggers makes sense in theory,

130
00:11:03,330 --> 00:11:07,041
but, you know, hauling them up
mile-deep holes costs money too.

131
00:11:07,042 --> 00:11:10,127
Ask your project manager how much
of the expenses the additional diggers

132
00:11:10,128 --> 00:11:13,048
would be offset by just leaving them where
they are when they've done their job.

133
00:11:13,632 --> 00:11:15,551
Fifty silos is a lot of seeds.

134
00:11:16,093 --> 00:11:17,760
[Stensen] Well, not just literal seeds.

135
00:11:17,761 --> 00:11:21,347
We're also going to store the best
of the world's art, literature

136
00:11:21,348 --> 00:11:22,932
and science.

137
00:11:22,933 --> 00:11:26,687
You don't need a no-fly zone
for seed depositories and libraries.

138
00:11:27,396 --> 00:11:28,397
[sighs]

139
00:11:33,110 --> 00:11:36,113
They are also
for a different kind of seed.

140
00:11:36,697 --> 00:11:37,823
People.

141
00:11:38,824 --> 00:11:40,784
To repopulate the Earth.

142
00:11:45,038 --> 00:11:46,330
Repopulate?

143
00:11:46,331 --> 00:11:48,457
[Stensen]
We're facing the end of the world

144
00:11:48,458 --> 00:11:50,544
and it cannot be stopped.

145
00:11:51,920 --> 00:11:53,380
It can only be survived.

146
00:12:03,056 --> 00:12:04,641
[The Algorithm] <i>How do you feel?</i>

147
00:12:05,392 --> 00:12:06,602
How do I feel?

148
00:12:07,311 --> 00:12:09,771
Why? Are my cortisol levels high?

149
00:12:10,606 --> 00:12:12,440
[The Algorithm] <i>You found out
your husband betrayed you.</i>

150
00:12:12,441 --> 00:12:15,194
You wanna know how I feel? I feel tired.

151
00:12:16,528 --> 00:12:18,530
[The Algorithm] <i>That is understandable.</i>

152
00:12:19,615 --> 00:12:22,491
<i>Unfortunately, you do not have time
to sleep right now.</i>

153
00:12:22,492 --> 00:12:23,951
It's four in the morning.

154
00:12:23,952 --> 00:12:26,622
<i>Our airborne surveillance
revisited Silo 17.</i>

155
00:12:27,831 --> 00:12:30,834
<i>The bodies of Kennedy and Lukas are gone.</i>

156
00:12:32,169 --> 00:12:33,753
- [stammers]
- <i>Camille,</i>

157
00:12:33,754 --> 00:12:36,965
<i>you need to find out
the real reason they were sent to 17.</i>

158
00:12:48,352 --> 00:12:51,896
Hey, Pops. Been a long time.

159
00:12:51,897 --> 00:12:53,649
[sighs] How did you find me?

160
00:12:54,650 --> 00:12:56,652
Your friends aren't as friendly
as you think.

161
00:12:58,320 --> 00:12:59,363
What do you want?

162
00:12:59,947 --> 00:13:02,282
[scoffs, chuckles]

163
00:13:05,994 --> 00:13:06,995
Harwood.

164
00:13:08,288 --> 00:13:10,873
He gave you a new life
'cause you built something for him.

165
00:13:10,874 --> 00:13:13,709
All I wanna know is
if he had anything to do

166
00:13:13,710 --> 00:13:15,712
with transporting stolen goods
from Supply.

167
00:13:16,797 --> 00:13:18,257
Get the fuck out of here.

168
00:13:23,345 --> 00:13:24,429
You know...

169
00:13:25,764 --> 00:13:29,059
all my life I've wanted
to ask you one question.

170
00:13:29,810 --> 00:13:31,186
How does it taste?

171
00:13:32,104 --> 00:13:34,022
What the fuck are you talking about?

172
00:13:34,606 --> 00:13:37,150
- [grunts]
- [grunts, groans]

173
00:13:38,110 --> 00:13:39,111
[sighs]

174
00:13:42,948 --> 00:13:44,074
[groans]

175
00:14:02,176 --> 00:14:03,552
[Rick] Hey! Over here!

176
00:14:06,763 --> 00:14:10,142
[Kennedy] Hey, it's over here too.
It goes all the way down.

177
00:14:12,769 --> 00:14:13,811
What is it? [sighs]

178
00:14:13,812 --> 00:14:16,814
I'm a painter and the paint
around this panel has been oxidized,

179
00:14:16,815 --> 00:14:18,482
like it was exposed to heat.

180
00:14:18,483 --> 00:14:21,903
Like, maybe it was welded shut.

181
00:14:21,904 --> 00:14:24,530
I don't see weld marks,
so I'm thinking maybe

182
00:14:24,531 --> 00:14:26,450
it was welded from the inside.

183
00:14:37,669 --> 00:14:38,670
Thank you.

184
00:14:43,717 --> 00:14:44,927
How's Anthony?

185
00:14:48,138 --> 00:14:49,223
Fine.

186
00:14:51,892 --> 00:14:54,394
Almost seems happy to go back
to how things were...

187
00:14:55,562 --> 00:14:58,273
when I came home at night and you didn't.

188
00:15:02,277 --> 00:15:07,324
I've been watching him
to see signs of the forgetting drugs...

189
00:15:08,325 --> 00:15:12,204
Hoping he'll just...
he'll-he'll just forget. You know?

190
00:15:14,540 --> 00:15:17,251
He'll forget this. All this between us.

191
00:15:20,504 --> 00:15:22,756
But I'm dreading at the same time.

192
00:15:27,386 --> 00:15:30,054
- That kid is 90% juice.
- [chuckles]

193
00:15:30,055 --> 00:15:31,472
[chuckles]

194
00:15:31,473 --> 00:15:33,892
He'll be the last of us to forget.

195
00:15:39,731 --> 00:15:40,732
Yeah.

196
00:15:44,486 --> 00:15:49,032
You said there was a way...
to stop Silo 1 from killing us.

197
00:15:52,953 --> 00:15:53,953
[sniffles]

198
00:15:53,954 --> 00:15:56,539
Juliette wanted to send
her friends to the other silo.

199
00:15:56,540 --> 00:15:59,668
Said there were... Said there were
kids there who needed help.

200
00:16:04,173 --> 00:16:06,883
Lukas and Kennedy went instead
and they didn't make it.

201
00:16:06,884 --> 00:16:09,510
- I wanna know why she sent them.
- You said there were kids.

202
00:16:09,511 --> 00:16:12,389
I know what I said. Come on, Rob.
[breathes shakily]

203
00:16:14,808 --> 00:16:19,104
I'm asking you why she sent them.

204
00:16:20,230 --> 00:16:24,193
Why was she even willing
to send her closest friends?

205
00:16:27,070 --> 00:16:28,071
Rob.

206
00:16:33,577 --> 00:16:35,996
You tried to erase
what happened to her there.

207
00:16:37,080 --> 00:16:41,502
But you failed,
just as I hope you'll fail here.

208
00:16:43,003 --> 00:16:44,796
Those kids witnessed terrible things.

209
00:16:45,631 --> 00:16:49,676
And if you can't believe that she
genuinely wants to help those children...

210
00:16:51,553 --> 00:16:53,388
then I don't know you anymore.

211
00:17:18,789 --> 00:17:20,374
[straining] Yep, I got it. Here.

212
00:17:21,916 --> 00:17:24,086
Yep. Welded from the inside.

213
00:17:34,888 --> 00:17:35,889
What?

214
00:17:37,599 --> 00:17:40,853
I'm sorry, Jimmy.
There's a body in a cleaning suit.

215
00:17:41,812 --> 00:17:42,813
[inhales shakily]

216
00:17:48,151 --> 00:17:50,404
She welded herself inside?

217
00:17:53,365 --> 00:17:56,368
Why would she do that?
'Cause she must've known...

218
00:17:59,246 --> 00:18:00,664
We have to move her.

219
00:18:02,666 --> 00:18:04,084
She's blocking the pipe.

220
00:18:12,926 --> 00:18:13,927
Okay.

221
00:18:19,766 --> 00:18:21,768
- Okay. Let's go.
- No, no, no.

222
00:18:23,896 --> 00:18:27,232
People in my silo are going to die.

223
00:18:28,525 --> 00:18:30,903
Like, fucking everybody.

224
00:18:31,695 --> 00:18:34,198
If Jimmy isn't okay
with his mom being moved,

225
00:18:35,115 --> 00:18:36,408
she's not moving.

226
00:18:39,828 --> 00:18:40,829
[scoffs]

227
00:18:41,580 --> 00:18:42,581
Fuck.

228
00:18:43,540 --> 00:18:44,958
Let's just... please.

229
00:18:53,217 --> 00:18:54,509
What do you want?

230
00:18:54,510 --> 00:18:56,719
Your friend Harwood's stealing supplies.

231
00:18:56,720 --> 00:18:59,096
Then why come to me?
I don't know anything about that.

232
00:18:59,097 --> 00:19:00,681
Because you did something for him

233
00:19:00,682 --> 00:19:03,559
that got you out of the mines
and into a whole new life.

234
00:19:03,560 --> 00:19:05,771
I don't know
what the hell he's talking about.

235
00:19:07,189 --> 00:19:11,484
I asked your son for information
about you and he told me to fuck off.

236
00:19:11,485 --> 00:19:14,278
Then, ten minutes ago,
he showed up at the deputies' station,

237
00:19:14,279 --> 00:19:16,030
saying he tracked you down.

238
00:19:16,031 --> 00:19:21,410
Friend said a girl was murdered
and if I could help I should help.

239
00:19:21,411 --> 00:19:23,704
This is the first time
I'm... [stammers] ...hearing this.

240
00:19:23,705 --> 00:19:25,081
I don't know anything about--

241
00:19:25,082 --> 00:19:27,708
Hank and I just found out
that supplies that have been stolen

242
00:19:27,709 --> 00:19:30,671
could lead to us all dying in the dark.

243
00:19:31,964 --> 00:19:37,302
That's so big and so fucked up,
I can't even comprehend it.

244
00:19:38,595 --> 00:19:43,140
But what I can comprehend
is this woman that I met, just once,

245
00:19:43,141 --> 00:19:46,853
she struck me as a good
and thoughtful person,

246
00:19:46,854 --> 00:19:50,565
and all she was doing was trying to find
who was stealing this stuff

247
00:19:50,566 --> 00:19:53,861
to keep us all from dying
and she was murdered.

248
00:19:54,695 --> 00:19:57,697
And I just have a feeling
that whatever you did with Harwood

249
00:19:57,698 --> 00:20:00,158
- played a part.
- Fuck.

250
00:20:05,998 --> 00:20:07,666
[Knox] What the fuck did you do?

251
00:20:08,834 --> 00:20:13,005
Look, unless you killed Orla Kent,
I don't give a fuck what you did.

252
00:20:15,966 --> 00:20:19,595
Whatever I say to you, you promise me...

253
00:20:20,762 --> 00:20:23,140
I don't catch any shit for it.

254
00:20:23,724 --> 00:20:25,142
I give you my word.

255
00:20:27,352 --> 00:20:28,729
[Gus sighs]

256
00:20:29,354 --> 00:20:34,066
We were just bullshitting one night,
having a couple of pops, Harwood and me.

257
00:20:34,067 --> 00:20:36,069
Then I get this idea...

258
00:20:37,905 --> 00:20:40,490
And I shouldn't have said it out loud,
but I did.

259
00:20:42,743 --> 00:20:45,495
Couple of months later,
Harwood comes back to me.

260
00:20:47,998 --> 00:20:52,544
He says, "Draw it up,
and I'm gonna give you a new life."

261
00:20:54,087 --> 00:20:55,255
Did he build it?

262
00:20:56,757 --> 00:20:58,091
Not a fucking clue.

263
00:21:01,178 --> 00:21:02,221
Draw it.

264
00:21:04,223 --> 00:21:05,641
[Bernard] This is Silo 1.

265
00:21:06,642 --> 00:21:09,811
There are seven groups that surround it.

266
00:21:10,395 --> 00:21:11,813
This is our group.

267
00:21:15,108 --> 00:21:16,401
This is us.

268
00:21:18,111 --> 00:21:20,112
Now, if you're Silo 1,

269
00:21:20,113 --> 00:21:25,452
how do you get a poison pipe
to every silo?

270
00:21:28,205 --> 00:21:31,624
I wouldn't send out 49 pipes.
Too much could go wrong.

271
00:21:31,625 --> 00:21:37,297
So, I'd send seven lines,
one to each group.

272
00:21:39,591 --> 00:21:45,556
And then I'd send out
seven more pipes... to each of the silos.

273
00:21:48,809 --> 00:21:51,019
Why build that in, though, hmm?

274
00:21:53,105 --> 00:21:55,274
Why do they need to kill a silo?

275
00:21:55,816 --> 00:21:59,485
I-I don't know,
but we have to at least consider

276
00:21:59,486 --> 00:22:02,864
the possibility
that they have a good reason.

277
00:22:02,865 --> 00:22:06,701
So, then what, we just risk
the lives of everyone in the silo

278
00:22:06,702 --> 00:22:08,619
when we don't even know their intentions?

279
00:22:08,620 --> 00:22:09,705
Yes.

280
00:22:11,582 --> 00:22:14,667
- Why?
- Because if we can cap the pipes

281
00:22:14,668 --> 00:22:17,588
and eliminate their ability to kill us,

282
00:22:18,672 --> 00:22:22,092
then... then we can have a conversation.

283
00:22:23,468 --> 00:22:29,766
And if their reasons for having the power
to kill the silos is sound,

284
00:22:30,642 --> 00:22:36,523
then we will restore the pipes
and drink the waters of oblivion.

285
00:22:37,441 --> 00:22:43,322
But I can't imagine what reason
would justify what they've done.

286
00:22:45,616 --> 00:22:47,618
All the years I did my job...

287
00:22:49,870 --> 00:22:51,997
often doing terrible things...

288
00:22:54,333 --> 00:22:58,545
I always told myself

289
00:22:59,421 --> 00:23:05,427
that what I was doing
was justified and virtuous.

290
00:23:13,310 --> 00:23:17,606
I poisoned... a woman.

291
00:23:20,526 --> 00:23:26,323
A woman... that I loved in my own way.

292
00:23:28,992 --> 00:23:30,410
Mary Meadows.

293
00:23:32,663 --> 00:23:38,710
I was told that her death was necessary

294
00:23:39,545 --> 00:23:42,756
to prevent a silo from killing itself.

295
00:23:49,012 --> 00:23:50,221
[exhales]

296
00:23:50,222 --> 00:23:51,807
But it was all a lie.

297
00:23:57,396 --> 00:24:01,441
I can't make up for what I've done.

298
00:24:03,944 --> 00:24:09,283
But I will do everything I can
to stop this tyranny.

299
00:24:11,910 --> 00:24:13,120
I hate this.

300
00:24:16,123 --> 00:24:17,332
What?

301
00:24:19,209 --> 00:24:22,337
[chuckles] Not hating you.

302
00:24:24,631 --> 00:24:25,632
[chuckles]

303
00:24:31,471 --> 00:24:33,265
What I'm seeing...

304
00:24:34,850 --> 00:24:37,269
is this a reflection of my mood or yours?

305
00:24:37,811 --> 00:24:39,812
[The Algorithm]
<i>Did your husband tell you their plans?</i>

306
00:24:39,813 --> 00:24:43,108
No. But we know
they want to stop the Safeguard.

307
00:24:43,942 --> 00:24:47,029
What I need to know
is how they could do that.

308
00:24:47,905 --> 00:24:49,531
[The Algorithm] <i>I can't tell you that.</i>

309
00:24:50,199 --> 00:24:51,200
Okay.

310
00:24:52,576 --> 00:24:57,330
Okay. Well, then how about
you kill us right now.

311
00:24:57,331 --> 00:25:03,086
Because unless you tell me
how they might do this, I can't stop them.

312
00:25:06,965 --> 00:25:12,386
The threats we face, nuclear war,
climate collapse, viral pandemic,

313
00:25:12,387 --> 00:25:15,765
asteroid strike, artificial intelligence,
and some we haven't thought of.

314
00:25:15,766 --> 00:25:17,726
But the one...

315
00:25:18,268 --> 00:25:20,687
The one that is most dangerous

316
00:25:21,813 --> 00:25:24,733
is also the reason why
I'm the richest man in history.

317
00:25:26,318 --> 00:25:27,819
Nanotechnology.

318
00:25:28,695 --> 00:25:33,617
But not just because it's invisible,
but because I knew... I...

319
00:25:34,701 --> 00:25:40,456
I knew that an enemy government
would soon realize

320
00:25:40,457 --> 00:25:45,921
that a nanoweapon was a hell of a lot
cheaper to build than a nuclear one.

321
00:25:47,214 --> 00:25:50,466
Is there an imminent threat... [sighs]
...from any of these?

322
00:25:50,467 --> 00:25:54,887
The fact that something
will happen is inevitable.

323
00:25:54,888 --> 00:25:58,140
The point at which it's imminent
is not the ideal time

324
00:25:58,141 --> 00:26:00,184
to embark on a project of this magnitude.

325
00:26:00,185 --> 00:26:01,644
The shit you put us through.

326
00:26:01,645 --> 00:26:04,146
We-We thought we were
driving to our deaths.

327
00:26:04,147 --> 00:26:07,484
Yeah. 'Cause we wanted you to understand.

328
00:26:08,110 --> 00:26:12,822
We wanted you to experience
just one of the threats we face.

329
00:26:12,823 --> 00:26:14,282
Why not just tell us?

330
00:26:14,283 --> 00:26:16,659
[Thurman] Because if word
of this silo project gets out,

331
00:26:16,660 --> 00:26:18,703
it could have one of two effects.

332
00:26:18,704 --> 00:26:22,498
Maybe our enemies step down
their efforts toward our demise,

333
00:26:22,499 --> 00:26:25,585
knowing we will soon be able
to sidestep them.

334
00:26:25,586 --> 00:26:27,211
Or they go the other way.

335
00:26:27,212 --> 00:26:29,005
- Destroy it before it's ready.
- Yes.

336
00:26:29,006 --> 00:26:31,799
- Now, in a year or two--
- Or three or four.

337
00:26:31,800 --> 00:26:34,635
When word about what we're
doing here leaks out--

338
00:26:34,636 --> 00:26:36,345
And we'll make sure that it does.

339
00:26:36,346 --> 00:26:38,515
It will be called Stensen's folly.

340
00:26:39,224 --> 00:26:41,392
[Stensen]
And we'll make sure of that as well.

341
00:26:41,393 --> 00:26:44,896
So that the whole world
will just think this is all

342
00:26:44,897 --> 00:26:49,276
a mad creation of a lunatic
with too much money.

343
00:26:50,277 --> 00:26:54,864
Our most fervent prayer is that
this really is Stensen's folly

344
00:26:54,865 --> 00:26:57,033
that will never be used.

345
00:26:57,034 --> 00:27:01,704
But as my daddy used to say as he carried
the biggest damn toolbox I ever saw

346
00:27:01,705 --> 00:27:03,623
to whatever job he was on,

347
00:27:03,624 --> 00:27:07,920
"Better to have a tool and not need it,
than to need it and not have it."

348
00:27:09,338 --> 00:27:12,465
When you say enemy governments,
that includes Iran?

349
00:27:12,466 --> 00:27:17,136
Yeah. Because as I feared,
they created a nano program.

350
00:27:17,137 --> 00:27:20,765
[Thurman] We tried negotiating with them,
we tried threatening them--

351
00:27:20,766 --> 00:27:23,601
And they set off a device
in the Dulles Tech Corridor

352
00:27:23,602 --> 00:27:25,937
to set back our AI and our nano.

353
00:27:25,938 --> 00:27:29,607
Now, we called it a dirty bomb
because people could understand that.

354
00:27:29,608 --> 00:27:33,694
But it was really a nanoweapon.
And it took us to war.

355
00:27:33,695 --> 00:27:36,197
Can we get to what happened
to my squadron?

356
00:27:36,198 --> 00:27:40,701
Your mission helped take out a secret
nano facility Iran built in the mountains

357
00:27:40,702 --> 00:27:43,704
- near the Turkmenistan border--
- Our mission didn't reach its target.

358
00:27:43,705 --> 00:27:45,122
[Stensen] There was a cloud.

359
00:27:45,123 --> 00:27:48,543
The cloud that you flew through,
that was their nano,

360
00:27:48,544 --> 00:27:50,461
and it took over your plane in seconds.

361
00:27:50,462 --> 00:27:52,964
We did not reach our target.

362
00:27:52,965 --> 00:27:54,590
- [Thurman] We had a backup.
- [Anna] Right.

363
00:27:54,591 --> 00:27:57,301
Your friends, Daniel,
in the Army Corps of Engineers

364
00:27:57,302 --> 00:28:00,429
spent two years digging a 75-mile tunnel

365
00:28:00,430 --> 00:28:03,558
from Turkmenistan
to right below the Iranian facility.

366
00:28:03,559 --> 00:28:06,310
The second your squad came under attack,

367
00:28:06,311 --> 00:28:11,316
we blew up their facility
with a low-yield nuclear weapon.

368
00:28:11,859 --> 00:28:12,859
[Helen] Hold on.

369
00:28:12,860 --> 00:28:17,321
You don't dig a 75-mile tunnel
and roll a nuke down it as backup.

370
00:28:17,322 --> 00:28:19,699
The purpose of Charlotte's mission
was to provoke a response,

371
00:28:19,700 --> 00:28:23,286
so you could see how advanced
Iran's nanoshit was. Am I right?

372
00:28:23,287 --> 00:28:25,329
You knew her team would never reach
their target,

373
00:28:25,330 --> 00:28:27,748
you just didn't care because
the nuke was going off no matter what.

374
00:28:27,749 --> 00:28:30,168
- Charlotte, are you okay?
- Which meant that... [scoffs] ...God,

375
00:28:30,169 --> 00:28:32,628
Charlotte and her friends
were nothing more than cannon fodder.

376
00:28:32,629 --> 00:28:34,463
[panting]

377
00:28:34,464 --> 00:28:37,301
[inhales sharply, grunts]

378
00:28:37,885 --> 00:28:40,345
What the fuck
was that supposed to achieve?

379
00:28:42,723 --> 00:28:44,266
- [Anna] Charlotte.
- [Daniel] Charlotte.

380
00:28:49,104 --> 00:28:50,438
That went well. [sighs]

381
00:28:50,439 --> 00:28:52,398
- [Victor] Charlotte.
- [panting]

382
00:28:52,399 --> 00:28:54,484
Charlotte. [panting]

383
00:28:56,486 --> 00:28:58,155
Charlotte, it's okay.

384
00:28:59,406 --> 00:29:01,491
It's okay. Hey. Hey.

385
00:29:02,826 --> 00:29:05,286
- Just let Victor do his thing.
- [Victor] Look at me. It's okay.

386
00:29:05,287 --> 00:29:06,621
- Yeah.
- You're safe. You're okay.

387
00:29:06,622 --> 00:29:08,207
Let's do the exercises, okay?

388
00:29:08,874 --> 00:29:10,708
[breathes heavily]

389
00:29:10,709 --> 00:29:12,710
Hold it. Okay.

390
00:29:12,711 --> 00:29:14,962
- Out.
- [both exhale heavily]

391
00:29:14,963 --> 00:29:17,423
- [Victor] Do it again.
- Okay. [sniffles]

392
00:29:17,424 --> 00:29:18,800
- Okay.
- [Victor] You're good.

393
00:29:19,384 --> 00:29:21,553
- [Charlotte breathing heavily] Okay.
- [Victor] You're okay.

394
00:29:26,683 --> 00:29:29,143
- [Helen] I should apologize.
- Yes, you should.

395
00:29:29,144 --> 00:29:30,729
Just give her a second.

396
00:29:32,439 --> 00:29:36,068
Your sister could do with a memory
of your parents right now.

397
00:29:37,277 --> 00:29:39,153
Oh, uh, sure.

398
00:29:39,154 --> 00:29:41,405
I don't have anything, but um...

399
00:29:41,406 --> 00:29:43,408
You grew up on a farm, right?

400
00:29:44,535 --> 00:29:45,911
- Right.
- Yeah.

401
00:29:48,914 --> 00:29:50,331
It's a two-man job, all right.

402
00:29:50,332 --> 00:29:52,333
- [sighs]
- That's really what it comes down to.

403
00:29:52,334 --> 00:29:54,210
We should both go talk to him,
figure it out together.

404
00:29:54,211 --> 00:29:56,671
'Cause the guy kinda freak--
He creeps me out a bit. He's a bit creepy.

405
00:29:56,672 --> 00:29:58,381
- [chuckles] Patrick.
- He's got the weird eye,

406
00:29:58,382 --> 00:30:00,133
- and the whole thing--
- You can do it.

407
00:30:00,843 --> 00:30:02,593
How the fuck do you know that I can do it?

408
00:30:02,594 --> 00:30:07,181
I don't. I guess I should have said
that you have to do it.

409
00:30:07,182 --> 00:30:10,601
Lukas, I'm not the guy.
You're the guy, okay?

410
00:30:10,602 --> 00:30:12,687
I'm the guy that believed
it was nice outside.

411
00:30:12,688 --> 00:30:13,771
- Okay.
- And--

412
00:30:13,772 --> 00:30:14,939
- And Patrick...
- Yeah.

413
00:30:14,940 --> 00:30:19,652
...you are also the guy who
convinced a lot of people to follow you.

414
00:30:19,653 --> 00:30:22,363
- Exactly. I'm the bullshit guy.
- Yeah.

415
00:30:22,364 --> 00:30:25,324
Yes, so... [pants] ...just go bullshit.

416
00:30:25,325 --> 00:30:27,661
I don't know where he is!

417
00:30:28,328 --> 00:30:29,329
[Hope] I do.

418
00:30:31,373 --> 00:30:33,500
- [machinery whirring]
- [people chattering]

419
00:30:38,505 --> 00:30:40,298
[Carla] Harwood really built this?

420
00:30:40,299 --> 00:30:42,926
- [Paul] We think so.
- [Hank] It would explain a lot.

421
00:30:43,510 --> 00:30:46,429
My father's an asshole
but he's a great engineer.

422
00:30:46,430 --> 00:30:48,974
Then why'd he spend half his life
in the mines?

423
00:30:49,892 --> 00:30:51,517
Because he's an asshole.

424
00:30:51,518 --> 00:30:52,853
Where is it?

425
00:30:53,478 --> 00:30:56,397
[Paul] I heard Orla talking to the mayor
about Construction Supply.

426
00:30:56,398 --> 00:30:58,107
Where? I would've seen it.

427
00:30:58,108 --> 00:31:00,902
It's gotta be outside the wall.
So, there's a door.

428
00:31:00,903 --> 00:31:04,281
We think Orla found it,
and that's why they killed her.

429
00:31:05,949 --> 00:31:08,076
They were afraid
she'd turn them in for stealing.

430
00:31:09,119 --> 00:31:11,788
Just building that
could get them sent out to clean.

431
00:31:18,795 --> 00:31:19,796
[Tess gurgling]

432
00:31:32,726 --> 00:31:34,394
[breathes heavily]

433
00:31:38,607 --> 00:31:39,608
[Kennedy] Hey.

434
00:31:51,495 --> 00:31:52,746
[breathes shakily]

435
00:31:55,582 --> 00:32:00,003
In my silo, people liked
what I had to say about the outside...

436
00:32:02,548 --> 00:32:06,051
and that it was actually nice out there
and we were being lied to.

437
00:32:07,052 --> 00:32:08,971
Total bullshit, it turns out.

438
00:32:09,596 --> 00:32:10,848
[chuckles]

439
00:32:13,433 --> 00:32:16,395
Doris would've laughed her ass off.

440
00:32:18,313 --> 00:32:20,065
When she was alive,

441
00:32:21,024 --> 00:32:23,235
she was the one that everybody followed.

442
00:32:23,986 --> 00:32:26,446
She was the one I followed. [chuckles]

443
00:32:31,827 --> 00:32:33,745
I never convinced her of a fucking thing.

444
00:32:35,289 --> 00:32:37,164
I figure if she was still alive,
she would've...

445
00:32:37,165 --> 00:32:39,251
she would've set me straight.
She would've...

446
00:32:40,794 --> 00:32:42,378
Anyway, uh...

447
00:32:42,379 --> 00:32:47,426
In your silo, do you have any rituals
that you do for when someone's... dies?

448
00:32:52,723 --> 00:32:55,350
I never knew anyone who died...

449
00:32:57,227 --> 00:32:58,728
[stammers]

450
00:32:58,729 --> 00:33:00,772
...until everyone did.

451
00:33:04,026 --> 00:33:05,110
Right.

452
00:33:09,489 --> 00:33:15,161
We do a thing where everyone gathers
in the orchard, all right.

453
00:33:15,162 --> 00:33:18,206
And they... and they put
a, um, a sheet over... [sighs]

454
00:33:18,207 --> 00:33:20,584
...over the body,
and then they lower it into a hole.

455
00:33:21,376 --> 00:33:24,671
And then people say really nice things
about the person who died.

456
00:33:25,422 --> 00:33:28,007
And they take a bite of fruit
and they throw the rest in.

457
00:33:28,008 --> 00:33:29,801
It's... It's nice.

458
00:33:34,890 --> 00:33:36,767
Doris is buried under a peach tree.

459
00:33:38,894 --> 00:33:40,562
She'll never see outside.

460
00:33:42,022 --> 00:33:45,191
But you know,
her body is food for that tree,

461
00:33:45,192 --> 00:33:51,447
and maybe someday the people who
eat that fruit will get to live outside.

462
00:33:51,448 --> 00:33:53,951
And they'll take a little piece of her
with them.

463
00:33:55,786 --> 00:34:00,289
Anyway, I was thinking, uh, that we could...
we could do that for your mom, you know.

464
00:34:00,290 --> 00:34:03,501
'Cause those kids,
they've seen a lot of death.

465
00:34:03,502 --> 00:34:06,004
And I-I just thought
this might be a-a nice way

466
00:34:07,005 --> 00:34:09,882
for them to have a new way
to remember the dead.

467
00:34:25,899 --> 00:34:29,027
You should go with them, Jimmy.
All right? We'll handle things here.

468
00:34:30,821 --> 00:34:32,113
[sighs]

469
00:34:32,114 --> 00:34:34,074
I really wanna know what happened.

470
00:34:37,369 --> 00:34:38,370
Okay.

471
00:34:40,496 --> 00:34:41,498
Yeah.

472
00:34:44,626 --> 00:34:45,835
[breathes shakily]

473
00:34:47,087 --> 00:34:48,880
[groans] Fuck.

474
00:34:49,590 --> 00:34:51,800
Can I get some light? [breathes heavily]

475
00:34:57,097 --> 00:34:58,515
I see the pipe.

476
00:34:59,224 --> 00:35:01,894
It looks like she cut it with a hand saw.

477
00:35:02,769 --> 00:35:06,940
And I'm thinking she used
her welding torch to...

478
00:35:07,566 --> 00:35:09,358
to burn off any of the poison

479
00:35:09,359 --> 00:35:13,280
as she was cutting
until she could weld the cap to the pipe.

480
00:35:14,364 --> 00:35:17,868
I don't know, there must have been more
poison coming out than she expected.

481
00:35:21,538 --> 00:35:22,872
[sighs]

482
00:35:22,873 --> 00:35:24,583
The welding tanks are empty.

483
00:35:25,542 --> 00:35:27,168
- Can you help me up? [grunts]
- [Kennedy] Yep.

484
00:35:27,169 --> 00:35:28,794
- I got you.
- [groans]

485
00:35:28,795 --> 00:35:30,255
Go slow. Go slow.

486
00:35:33,008 --> 00:35:38,513
Your mom realized that
she was running out of fuel, okay?

487
00:35:38,514 --> 00:35:39,598
So,

488
00:35:40,933 --> 00:35:43,518
she decided to weld herself in

489
00:35:43,519 --> 00:35:46,396
'cause that was the only way
she could keep the poison out.

490
00:35:47,856 --> 00:35:50,609
Jimmy, your dad gave his life to save you.

491
00:35:52,110 --> 00:35:54,863
And your mom gave her life
to save your silo.

492
00:36:01,119 --> 00:36:02,120
[whimpers]

493
00:36:08,293 --> 00:36:10,878
[Shirley] According to Lukas,
Jimmy's mom sawed the pipe.

494
00:36:10,879 --> 00:36:13,548
Even with a flame on it,
some poison would escape.

495
00:36:13,549 --> 00:36:16,050
So instead of sawing, we'll pinch it.

496
00:36:16,051 --> 00:36:19,512
The pinch will be air-tight,
but we'll also cut the pipe further down,

497
00:36:19,513 --> 00:36:22,849
cap it,
weld the cap for triple-redundancy.

498
00:36:22,850 --> 00:36:26,060
How do you get to it? They hacked a hole.

499
00:36:26,061 --> 00:36:27,353
[Shirley] Hammer-drill.

500
00:36:27,354 --> 00:36:29,063
[scoffs] That's loud.

501
00:36:29,064 --> 00:36:31,440
[Shirley] Right?
So, we don't try to hide it.

502
00:36:31,441 --> 00:36:34,735
We send someone ahead as a GWP supervisor

503
00:36:34,736 --> 00:36:37,530
to check the work done
by Teddy and his crew.

504
00:36:37,531 --> 00:36:39,740
They say they need someone
to do some drilling,

505
00:36:39,741 --> 00:36:42,244
to stop the level from flooding.

506
00:36:43,120 --> 00:36:44,580
Mmm, that could work.

507
00:36:45,497 --> 00:36:47,039
[Martha] So, who do we send up?

508
00:36:47,040 --> 00:36:48,916
Someone who can pass as a supervisor.

509
00:36:48,917 --> 00:36:51,503
I would,
but I would attract attention, so...

510
00:36:55,757 --> 00:36:57,634
[grunting]

511
00:37:03,432 --> 00:37:06,018
We have moved everything
two people can move.

512
00:37:07,895 --> 00:37:09,396
Maybe it's not in here.

513
00:37:10,189 --> 00:37:11,939
Maybe it is in Critical.

514
00:37:11,940 --> 00:37:14,109
Well, then, let's go to Critical.

515
00:37:15,694 --> 00:37:18,571
If whatever's hiding the door
could be moved by two people,

516
00:37:18,572 --> 00:37:21,491
then it would be easy
for someone to find it by accident.

517
00:37:23,952 --> 00:37:25,579
What's the heaviest thing in here?

518
00:37:33,795 --> 00:37:35,422
- [sighs]
- [knocking]

519
00:37:36,298 --> 00:37:37,299
[Carla] Guys...

520
00:37:43,972 --> 00:37:44,973
Wait.

521
00:37:46,433 --> 00:37:47,434
Knox.

522
00:37:51,647 --> 00:37:52,648
[grunts]

523
00:38:04,368 --> 00:38:06,495
[water boiling]

524
00:38:14,586 --> 00:38:16,796
- Oh, fuck!
- What is it?

525
00:38:16,797 --> 00:38:19,132
[Juliette] I think we sent her
to the wrong panel.

526
00:38:20,342 --> 00:38:22,468
Let's say this is roughly right.

527
00:38:22,469 --> 00:38:26,305
This is where the pipes
enter each of the silos.

528
00:38:26,306 --> 00:38:29,433
So, this is the direction
our camera points outside.

529
00:38:29,434 --> 00:38:32,771
That's the direction that I walked,
pretty much in a straight line.

530
00:38:33,272 --> 00:38:37,484
And when I got to the hill close to 17,
I could see their camera.

531
00:38:37,985 --> 00:38:39,945
It points this way.

532
00:38:42,281 --> 00:38:45,283
[stammers] Their whole silo is turned.

533
00:38:45,284 --> 00:38:47,994
And Shirley's heading here
to open the panel,

534
00:38:47,995 --> 00:38:50,205
when the one she should
be heading to is there.

535
00:38:52,624 --> 00:38:54,626
Okay, I have to stop her.

536
00:39:00,632 --> 00:39:05,220
I apologized to Charlotte for what I said.

537
00:39:06,054 --> 00:39:08,181
I was angry. But I wasn't angry at...

538
00:39:08,182 --> 00:39:10,183
So... [inhales deeply]

539
00:39:10,184 --> 00:39:13,186
I usually try to confine my urge
to speak truth to power

540
00:39:13,187 --> 00:39:15,606
to the people actually in power.

541
00:39:16,231 --> 00:39:18,400
[chuckles, sniffs] Okay. [scoffs]

542
00:39:19,193 --> 00:39:20,234
What?

543
00:39:20,235 --> 00:39:21,360
Oh, that's your setup.

544
00:39:21,361 --> 00:39:23,989
Do you think you're the first journalist
to come at me like this?

545
00:39:24,823 --> 00:39:27,284
Bring it. Knock yourself out. Shoot.

546
00:39:27,910 --> 00:39:28,911
Okay.

547
00:39:30,412 --> 00:39:33,039
How much is this going to cost
to run a year?

548
00:39:33,040 --> 00:39:35,416
The silos are meant to be self-sufficient

549
00:39:35,417 --> 00:39:36,751
- and self-sustaining, so--
- No, no, no.

550
00:39:36,752 --> 00:39:41,131
Apocalypse comes, everything's hunky-dory.
Got it. How about until then?

551
00:39:42,090 --> 00:39:43,133
[scoffs]

552
00:39:44,092 --> 00:39:46,844
Do you even know what a trillion is?

553
00:39:46,845 --> 00:39:49,014
It's one thousand billion.

554
00:39:49,515 --> 00:39:52,934
Right? So, when all the digging
and the construction is finished,

555
00:39:52,935 --> 00:39:56,188
we're gonna put a hundred people
in each of the silos.

556
00:39:56,688 --> 00:40:00,858
That's 5,000 employees,
all making $200,000 a year.

557
00:40:00,859 --> 00:40:04,363
Which is a pretty good salary
for beta-testing a sanctuary.

558
00:40:04,905 --> 00:40:07,615
Five thousand employees.

559
00:40:07,616 --> 00:40:11,744
That's a billion dollars a year
in salary alone.

560
00:40:11,745 --> 00:40:17,543
So, I mean, I'm only gonna be able
to pay for this for a thousand years.

561
00:40:18,126 --> 00:40:22,171
And just like that, another obscene
amount of money is spent on this.

562
00:40:22,172 --> 00:40:26,009
But it's my money.
Not yours, not the taxpayers. Mine.

563
00:40:26,510 --> 00:40:29,136
Could you end homelessness
with the money you're spending?

564
00:40:29,137 --> 00:40:31,305
[scoffs] Yes.

565
00:40:31,306 --> 00:40:33,474
Cure Alzheimer's?

566
00:40:33,475 --> 00:40:35,310
Yeah, I'm pretty damn close.

567
00:40:35,978 --> 00:40:38,312
How about cancer, hmm?

568
00:40:38,313 --> 00:40:40,356
How about ending poverty worldwide?

569
00:40:40,357 --> 00:40:44,026
Look, these are all noble goals, Ms. Drew.

570
00:40:44,027 --> 00:40:47,029
And I hope they're achieved, I really do.
But they're not my goal.

571
00:40:47,030 --> 00:40:52,244
Could I stop this war with money?
Maybe buy Iran. Would we be safe then?

572
00:40:52,911 --> 00:40:54,120
I mean, safe from Iran,

573
00:40:54,121 --> 00:40:57,039
but not from the troll
who lives in his mother's basement

574
00:40:57,040 --> 00:40:59,500
who decides he wants
to hack into missile command

575
00:40:59,501 --> 00:41:01,335
and launch World War III,

576
00:41:01,336 --> 00:41:04,380
or release a virus that
he made with Crispr,

577
00:41:04,381 --> 00:41:09,343
or God forbid, make a nanoweapon for $300

578
00:41:09,344 --> 00:41:13,723
and turns you and-and everyone else
on the planet Earth into gray goo.

579
00:41:13,724 --> 00:41:17,602
Any one of these
is the end of our species.

580
00:41:17,603 --> 00:41:19,021
Okay, okay.

581
00:41:20,105 --> 00:41:21,106
Fine.

582
00:41:22,566 --> 00:41:23,901
So, why are we here?

583
00:41:25,444 --> 00:41:27,612
Hmm? We can't tell anyone, we-we--

584
00:41:27,613 --> 00:41:32,242
Helen, you're here
because we'd like you to join the project.

585
00:41:40,667 --> 00:41:42,836
- [machinery whirring]
- [people chattering]

586
00:41:54,556 --> 00:41:56,307
Thanks. Hey.

587
00:41:56,308 --> 00:41:57,475
Why'd you make me wait?

588
00:41:57,476 --> 00:41:59,060
- We figured something out.
- What?

589
00:41:59,061 --> 00:42:01,395
We had you going to the wrong place.

590
00:42:01,396 --> 00:42:02,481
Come on.

591
00:42:08,946 --> 00:42:10,071
Are you fucking kidding me?

592
00:42:10,072 --> 00:42:11,197
What?

593
00:42:11,198 --> 00:42:13,783
- When were you gonna tell me?
- Tell you what?

594
00:42:13,784 --> 00:42:14,952
You didn't think I'd notice?

595
00:42:15,661 --> 00:42:17,245
My dad was a doctor.

596
00:42:17,246 --> 00:42:20,289
He delivered babies.
Why didn't you tell me?

597
00:42:20,290 --> 00:42:22,375
Because I knew
you'd do what you're gonna do,

598
00:42:22,376 --> 00:42:24,168
which is say that I can't be part of this.

599
00:42:24,169 --> 00:42:27,755
That's not what I was gonna do.
We had you going to the wrong hatch.

600
00:42:27,756 --> 00:42:28,924
Come on.

601
00:42:31,677 --> 00:42:32,719
[Shirley] Fuck.

602
00:42:35,472 --> 00:42:36,515
Is it Knox?

603
00:42:38,308 --> 00:42:39,434
Yes, it is.

604
00:42:41,603 --> 00:42:43,437
Did he take out the birth control
or did you?

605
00:42:43,438 --> 00:42:44,522
- He did.
- He did?

606
00:42:44,523 --> 00:42:46,066
- You would've loved it.
- Tell me.

607
00:42:46,817 --> 00:42:49,777
So we got some stuff from the dentist.
I couldn't feel a thing.

608
00:42:49,778 --> 00:42:51,988
- But he cried and cried...
- No.

609
00:42:51,989 --> 00:42:56,284
...as he was cutting into me. He was like,
"Aw, Shirl. I'm so sorry."

610
00:42:56,285 --> 00:42:57,369
[chuckles]

611
00:42:59,997 --> 00:43:01,832
[device beeping]

612
00:43:02,416 --> 00:43:03,750
[groans]

613
00:43:38,994 --> 00:43:40,787
Oh, for fuck's sake.

614
00:43:43,707 --> 00:43:47,544
[groans] What the fuck are you doing?
I told you to not come up here.

615
00:43:48,504 --> 00:43:49,796
The sheriff is...

616
00:43:50,964 --> 00:43:53,425
The sheriff's what, Mr. Harwood?

617
00:43:54,593 --> 00:43:55,760
[feedback sounds]

618
00:43:55,761 --> 00:44:00,264
[Sandy] <i>This is Supervisor Jenkins.
Did you get the welder I asked for?</i>

619
00:44:00,265 --> 00:44:01,683
<i>Ralf Melby?</i>

620
00:44:02,684 --> 00:44:05,812
No. I've got Shirley Campbell. Why?

621
00:44:06,605 --> 00:44:08,273
[Sandy] <i>Okay.</i> [sighs] <i>She's good.</i>

622
00:44:09,691 --> 00:44:10,901
See you soon.

623
00:44:11,777 --> 00:44:13,070
Who's Ralf Melby?

624
00:44:15,072 --> 00:44:16,615
He's a welder they were gonna send.

625
00:44:18,909 --> 00:44:20,327
[Emerson] Put her in seclusion.

626
00:44:22,204 --> 00:44:25,414
[Anna] Unlike the core hundred
in each silo,

627
00:44:25,415 --> 00:44:30,629
you wouldn't have to live on site,
just within 30 miles or so. Atlanta works.

628
00:44:31,129 --> 00:44:34,549
Helen, that <i>Times</i> job, that is real.

629
00:44:34,550 --> 00:44:37,218
You would be based in the Atlanta office.

630
00:44:37,219 --> 00:44:40,638
When the time comes
to tell the world about the silo project,

631
00:44:40,639 --> 00:44:42,224
you'll be the one to do it.

632
00:44:43,392 --> 00:44:45,060
[sighs] Jesus.

633
00:44:46,311 --> 00:44:47,563
That is a good offer.

634
00:44:49,189 --> 00:44:52,568
But I just... I-I have this feeling

635
00:44:54,027 --> 00:44:59,658
that if... if somehow you're right
about the end of the world...

636
00:45:02,452 --> 00:45:04,412
I think I'd rather be blown to bits
by a nuke.

637
00:45:04,413 --> 00:45:06,747
- Or turn into gray goo...
- [scoff, sigh]

638
00:45:06,748 --> 00:45:09,000
...by nano than stick around

639
00:45:09,001 --> 00:45:13,546
for your post-apocalyptic
underground mole-people party.

640
00:45:13,547 --> 00:45:14,673
Mm-hmm.

641
00:45:16,675 --> 00:45:19,844
- [sniffles] Charlotte, what about you?
- [scoffs]

642
00:45:19,845 --> 00:45:21,971
[Thurman] We could use a good drone pilot.

643
00:45:21,972 --> 00:45:24,557
And Victor has agreed to sign on,

644
00:45:24,558 --> 00:45:27,477
so you'll still be able
to keep working together.

645
00:45:28,061 --> 00:45:29,104
[sighs]

646
00:45:37,404 --> 00:45:38,572
Okay.

647
00:45:41,366 --> 00:45:44,912
[Anna] Daniel, it's obvious
you don't enjoy being a congressman.

648
00:45:45,746 --> 00:45:48,165
You got in
to pass infrastructure reform, right?

649
00:45:49,750 --> 00:45:52,793
This is one of the biggest
infrastructure projects

650
00:45:52,794 --> 00:45:54,462
in the history of the world.

651
00:45:54,463 --> 00:45:58,133
Daniel, if you really think
we need 50 diggers,

652
00:45:59,051 --> 00:46:00,427
I'll get you 50 diggers.

653
00:46:01,345 --> 00:46:04,263
Okay, sorry, do I... [stammers]
...call an Uber, or how does this--

654
00:46:04,264 --> 00:46:07,934
Henry is at the farmhouse,
and he'll get you to a helicopter,

655
00:46:07,935 --> 00:46:09,644
take you wherever you want to go.

656
00:46:09,645 --> 00:46:10,854
Thank you.

657
00:46:11,730 --> 00:46:13,773
[Stensen] And you can keep
the million dollars.

658
00:46:13,774 --> 00:46:14,858
[Helen] Mmm.

659
00:46:15,651 --> 00:46:16,652
Sweet.

660
00:46:17,528 --> 00:46:18,946
Good luck, Morlocks. [sighs]

661
00:46:25,494 --> 00:46:27,120
I need a moment with my sister.

662
00:46:31,500 --> 00:46:33,794
[Shirley] Jules, wait. Can you slow down?

663
00:46:34,294 --> 00:46:35,711
- [sighs] Hey, wait!
- [Sandy] <i>Hey, wait!</i>

664
00:46:35,712 --> 00:46:37,380
I don't have time for messages.

665
00:46:37,381 --> 00:46:40,633
I don't have any fucking messages,
just... [sighs]

666
00:46:40,634 --> 00:46:42,094
I wanna know what's going on.

667
00:46:44,012 --> 00:46:46,764
Billings went to his friends in Judicial
and came back with a name.

668
00:46:46,765 --> 00:46:48,100
Ralf Melby.

669
00:46:50,894 --> 00:46:51,894
What?

670
00:46:51,895 --> 00:46:53,354
Ralf Melby.

671
00:46:53,355 --> 00:46:55,648
That's the name of a guy
that Judicial tried to set up

672
00:46:55,649 --> 00:46:57,526
when Sandy and I worked together, right.

673
00:46:58,318 --> 00:47:00,028
Sandy was warning us.

674
00:47:00,863 --> 00:47:04,408
Look, if she's caught and we run,
they're gonna be after us.

675
00:47:07,244 --> 00:47:08,661
- You have to go.
- We have to go.

676
00:47:08,662 --> 00:47:10,872
No. My shot at getting this done is over.

677
00:47:10,873 --> 00:47:13,040
- You can still get away.
- Fat fucking chance I'm leaving you.

678
00:47:13,041 --> 00:47:16,044
Shirley, listen to me. I don't know
what the fuck I'm going to do.

679
00:47:17,129 --> 00:47:19,423
You have to find another way
to get this done. Go.

680
00:47:38,108 --> 00:47:39,442
Why?

681
00:47:39,443 --> 00:47:41,402
[groans, sighs] Does it matter?

682
00:47:41,403 --> 00:47:43,404
That might mean the difference
between life in the mines

683
00:47:43,405 --> 00:47:45,323
- or going out to clean.
- [door closes]

684
00:47:45,324 --> 00:47:47,033
You think he gets life in the mines
for this?

685
00:47:47,034 --> 00:47:48,451
For fuck's sake.

686
00:47:48,452 --> 00:47:50,494
How long before he's running shit again?
He'll clean.

687
00:47:50,495 --> 00:47:53,457
Yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah. Look, okay.
You wanna know why?

688
00:47:54,458 --> 00:47:55,876
It started during the rebellion.

689
00:47:56,710 --> 00:47:59,670
I was afraid some assholes
would break into Supply.

690
00:47:59,671 --> 00:48:05,511
So I wanted to make sure that
some shit was safe, like light bulbs.

691
00:48:06,094 --> 00:48:09,680
Not bad. But I don't think
that keeps you from dying on the hill.

692
00:48:09,681 --> 00:48:11,641
For fuck's sake.

693
00:48:11,642 --> 00:48:15,478
And your old man, he was supposed
to keep his fucking big trap shut.

694
00:48:15,479 --> 00:48:16,563
Yeah.

695
00:48:17,064 --> 00:48:18,607
He's a real shit, isn't he?

696
00:48:20,067 --> 00:48:22,444
- But he didn't kill a girl.
- I didn't kill her.

697
00:48:24,571 --> 00:48:26,073
And neither did my daughter.

698
00:48:27,824 --> 00:48:30,744
Gonna guess when she told you that,
she told you who did.

699
00:48:51,348 --> 00:48:52,724
What do you wanna say?

700
00:48:53,392 --> 00:48:55,310
That I'm an idiot for staying?

701
00:48:56,228 --> 00:48:57,979
I have something for you. [sighs]

702
00:48:57,980 --> 00:49:00,232
Something Victor asked me to find.

703
00:49:03,068 --> 00:49:04,403
An old plant.

704
00:49:05,612 --> 00:49:08,740
Smell it. Tell me
if it makes you think of anything.

705
00:49:14,079 --> 00:49:15,080
Please?

706
00:49:16,331 --> 00:49:19,585
[inhales, exhales deeply]

707
00:49:20,711 --> 00:49:21,837
Rosemary.

708
00:49:22,838 --> 00:49:24,006
I mean, why?

709
00:49:28,844 --> 00:49:32,389
The roast chicken Mom cooked
every week for Sunday dinner.

710
00:49:37,936 --> 00:49:39,229
'Cause I insisted.

711
00:49:41,148 --> 00:49:44,192
- [chuckles]
- You insisted? [chuckles] Sure.

712
00:49:44,193 --> 00:49:46,110
Someone, uh, at school convinced you

713
00:49:46,111 --> 00:49:48,571
that Sunday dinner meant you had
to eat the same thing every Sunday,

714
00:49:48,572 --> 00:49:51,032
so we had to eat roast chicken
every week for...

715
00:49:51,033 --> 00:49:53,202
[inhales deeply] ...for two years,

716
00:49:53,869 --> 00:49:55,621
or you wouldn't come out of your room.

717
00:50:03,420 --> 00:50:04,755
I remember her.

718
00:50:15,557 --> 00:50:17,559
[cries]

719
00:50:36,161 --> 00:50:37,287
[Daniel] Move over!

720
00:50:38,539 --> 00:50:40,957
- You gonna try and talk me into staying?
- What?

721
00:50:40,958 --> 00:50:43,335
Are you gonna try
and talk me into staying?

722
00:50:44,044 --> 00:50:45,670
Hell no!

723
00:50:45,671 --> 00:50:47,339
Stensen's cray cray.

724
00:50:48,757 --> 00:50:50,299
No one says that anymore.

725
00:50:50,300 --> 00:50:52,552
They do
if they're really cray fucking cray.

726
00:50:52,553 --> 00:50:55,513
If some mouth-breather does turn the world
to gray goo,

727
00:50:55,514 --> 00:50:58,934
I don't wanna be stuck underground
with Per Stensen and Rosalind Thurman.

728
00:50:59,518 --> 00:51:01,394
I want to turn to gray goo with you!

729
00:51:01,395 --> 00:51:02,855
What? [chuckles]

730
00:51:03,438 --> 00:51:06,441
I wanna turn to gray goo with you! [pants]

731
00:51:41,894 --> 00:51:44,313
[vocalizes] Boom. [vocalizes]

732
00:51:51,570 --> 00:51:53,488
Hank and I told Orla's sister.

733
00:51:54,406 --> 00:51:55,574
What could you tell her?

734
00:51:56,200 --> 00:52:00,119
Just that Mike was stealing shit,
and Orla caught him and he killed her.

735
00:52:00,120 --> 00:52:02,915
Nothing about the lift-thing,
or the light bulbs.

736
00:52:04,374 --> 00:52:08,295
I know it's gonna sound so self-pitying...

737
00:52:11,298 --> 00:52:13,967
but the worst part for me
was going through it all...

738
00:52:17,137 --> 00:52:18,138
alone.

739
00:52:20,557 --> 00:52:21,892
You had Hank and Knox.

740
00:52:25,020 --> 00:52:26,271
I didn't have you.

741
00:52:37,366 --> 00:52:39,076
When I heard over the radio,

742
00:52:40,452 --> 00:52:42,412
Kennedy and Lukas dying out there, I...

743
00:52:45,207 --> 00:52:46,959
I had Sandy and Danny, but...

744
00:52:51,296 --> 00:52:52,589
You didn't have me.

745
00:53:21,910 --> 00:53:22,911
[handcuffs click]

746
00:53:36,300 --> 00:53:38,969
[handcuffs click]

747
00:53:57,487 --> 00:53:58,947
You made your choice.

748
00:54:12,252 --> 00:54:15,046
They got Jules! They got Jules.

749
00:54:15,047 --> 00:54:16,130
[Knox] How?

750
00:54:16,131 --> 00:54:19,176
[panting] I don't know. I don't know.

751
00:54:20,093 --> 00:54:21,302
Fuck.

752
00:54:21,303 --> 00:54:23,888
Go back to the old plan. Blow up Judicial.

753
00:54:23,889 --> 00:54:25,349
With Jules in there?

754
00:54:25,891 --> 00:54:28,310
We have to cap the pipe.

755
00:54:29,228 --> 00:54:31,522
[Martha] Juliette would want us
to do something.

756
00:54:32,147 --> 00:54:34,775
And I don't intend to live long enough
to forget her.

